  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\n     TODAY THROUGH MONDAY EVENING\n\nEarly this morning, numerous showers and storms continue to \nlinger across portions of central, eastern, and southeast Kansas\nthanks to persistent mid-level WAA overspreading the area. \nWhile a couple of storms were able to produce strong wind gusts\nearlier, it seems as though the potential for severe wind gusts\nhas significantly decreased. The main concern this morning is \nthe potential for localized flooding from heavy rainfall. \nHowever, the coverage of heavy rainfall is expected to quickly \ndecrease as we go through the morning hours.\n\nAll of this rain and cloud cover will leave behind a residual \nboundary that is forecast to be somewhere between the KS/OK border \nto along the US-56 corridor this afternoon. While chances are \nlow, strong diurnal heating could be enough to support an \nuncapped environment, and sufficient convergence along this \nboundary may be enough to trigger a couple of isolated storms \nthis afternoon and early evening. This activity will be \nprimarily diurnally driven, so as convective inhibition builds \ntowards sunset, storm chances should decrease fairly quickly. \nIt's possible any storm that develops could be strong with small\nhail and gusty winds given MLCAPE between 2500-3000 J/kg. \nHowever, the intensity will be limited by marginal shear, \ngenerally in the neighborhood of 20 knots. In addition to the \nlow storm chances, hot conditions will return to portions of the\narea, mainly across south-central and southeast Kansas. With \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 90s, and dewpoints in the 70s, \nportions of southern and southeast Kansas could see heat indices\nexceed 100 this afternoon.\n\nThe hot airmass will expanding across much of the central plains \nheading into Monday thanks to potent southerly low level flow out \nahead of a slowly approaching cold front throughout the day. Due to \nan expected tight pressure gradient across the area, Monday \ncould be quite windy with sustained winds around 20 to 30 mph \nand gusts between 35 to 45 mph at times. The windiest conditions\nwill be along and west of I-135 during the afternoon hours. \nThese hot and windy conditions will contribute to a brief period\nof very high grassland fire danger west of I-135 during the \nafternoon hours on Monday; though dewpoints in the low 60s and \nrecent rainfall should mitigate fire weather concerns.\n\n     MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY\n\nOnly minimal changes in the forecast have been noted for this \nweek. As mentioned in previous forecasts, a strong deep layer \ntrough is forecast to traverse into the central/northern plains \nby Monday, and will be the catalyst for the aforementioned cold \nfront sagging into Kansas by Monday night. Mid-range guidance \ncontinues to suggest the frontal boundary will be draped across \nKansas from northeast to southwest through much of this week. \nSeveral perturbations embedded within zonal flow aloft will \nprovide the lift necessary for potentially several rounds of \nshowers and storms this week. Guidance is coming into better \nfocus with where the potential for heavy rainfall will be, and \nit will generally be along and north of I-70 Monday night \nthrough Wednesday where multiple rounds of training \nthunderstorms could potentially lead to flooding concerns. \nAdditionally, mid-range guidance is suggesting environmental \ninstability and shear may be enough to support a few strong to \nmarginally severe storms with large hail and damaging winds \nbeing the primary concern.\n\nMinimal adjustments have been made to the temperature forecast. \nAfternoon highs across the southern half of Kansas remain on the \nwarm to hot side of things with afternoon highs in the mid to upper \n90s, and afternoon heat indices near or slightly above 100. \nTemperatures have been raised slightly for Thursday as mid-range \nguidance has trended slower with the cold front pushing across \nKansas. However, cooler temperatures are still forecast to arrive by \nthis end of the week across the forecast area.",
